summary:
Event SummaryJean-François Kouassi, a young Ivorian political activist known by the acronym JFK and presenting himself as the “president of the youth,” was arrested on 8 January 2026, in Abidjan, in the 1st district of Plateau, following a police operation.
His arrest followed his outspoken posts on social media, in which he denounced irregularities in public service recruitment exams, including the alleged sale of customs positions and nepotistic practices within the Ministry of Public Service. Consequently, on 12 January 2026, he was brought before the public prosecutor and placed in pre-trial detention, charged with disturbing public order, defamation, dissemination of false information, and malicious denunciation.
Subsequently, on 26 January 2026, the Court of First Instance of Abidjan sentenced him to fifteen days of firm imprisonment, in addition to an eight-month suspended sentence and a fine of 500,000 CFA francs. He served his sentence and was released on 27 January 2026.
&nbsp;
&nbsp;

Event SummaryThe Colombian Ombudsman's Office warned that during January 2026, there were 12 incidents of mass forced displacement affecting 3,409 people, with Cauca being the department with the highest number of people affected, followed by Norte de Santander, Magdalena, and Antioquia. In addition, seven incidents of confinement were documented, restricting the mobility of 17,651 people in the country, mainly affecting communities in Caquetá, Cauca, Antioquia, Norte de Santander, Putumayo and Chocó. The organisation warned of the continuing humanitarian crisis associated with the armed conflict and the need to strengthen the institutional response to assist displaced or confined communities and prevent the overflow of territorial capacities.
In this pre-election context, rural communities: including indigenous peoples, Afro-descendant communities, and peasant communities; have faced severe restrictions on mobility, threats, and armed social control, which not only exacerbates the humanitarian crisis but also limits the exercise of fundamental rights such as citizen participation, social organisation, and access to basic services.
&nbsp;

Event SummaryOn the morning of 7 February 2026, the Casa dos Direitos - which serves as the headquarters of the Guinean League of Human Rights - was once again closed, and the Ambassador of the European Union, Mr. Federico Bianchi Antília, and the Attaché for Cooperation, Justice, Gender and Human Rights, Mr. Carlos Abaitua Zarza, while on a guided visit to the building, were unexpectedly expelled by a group of armed and hooded Rapid Intervention Police officers, without any explanation or legal grounds for such action. This sad episode follows the invasion of the Casa dos Direitos on 22 and 23 December 2025. It should be noted that the Casa dos Direitos Consortium is currently holding the Human Rights Fortnight, which was supposed to take place in December but was postponed to February due to the coup d’état that interrupted the general elections.
This event is a continuation of the context surrounding the general elections held on 23 November 2025, which were suspended by the military, allegedly as a measure to restore order, national security, stability, and to combat drug trafficking. Since the Casa dos Direitos hosts activities of civil society organizations, it has become a target of the dictatorial regime. In other words, the objective is to shut down the Casa dos Direitos as a way to reduce the Guinean League of Human Rights’ capacity to act, continue the persecution of organizations that criticize the regime, and silence all voices that denounce human rights violations, abuses of the law, and attacks on democracy.
&nbsp;
